    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d160">

                <pb facs="0193" n="181" xml:id="pg181"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">835.741/14: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-12-17T19:00:00-04:00">December 17, 1942—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1966. Your 2467, December 5, noon.<anchor n="16" xml:id="d160-f1"/> With
                    reference to the Department’s 1592, October 23, 4 p.m., the British Foreign
                    Office is agreeable to the prohibition of code messages to or from non-American
                    countries by radio, although it would under no circumstances agree to the
                    prohibition of code messages on cables used by the British.</p>
                <p>Please consult your British colleague and, unless you feel definitely that it
                    would be inadvisable, transmit Harbord’s message to Hayes with a view to having
                    Transradio take the proposed action immediately.</p>
                <p>You may tell Hayes, if you believe it desirable, that there is no objection to
                    informing the Argentine authorities of the contemplated action. It would appear
                    desirable, however, to allow the least possible time to elapse between
                    notification of the Argentine authorities and the contemplated action.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
